      "content": "<h3>The big idea: a HashSet is a HashMap</h3>\n<p>A <strong>HashSet does not have its own storage</strong>. Inside it holds a <strong>HashMap</strong>; every element you add becomes a <strong>key</strong> of that map, and the value is always the same dummy object. So \"a Set has no duplicates\" is really \"a Map has no duplicate keys\".</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Field: <code>private transient HashMap&lt;E, Object&gt; map;</code></li>\n<li>Field: <code>private static final Object PRESENT = new Object();</code> (the shared dummy value)</li>\n<li><code>add(e)</code> is <code>map.put(e, PRESENT) == null</code>: it returns <strong>true</strong> only if the key was new.</li>\n<li><code>contains(e)</code> is <code>map.containsKey(e)</code>; <code>remove(e)</code> is <code>map.remove(e) == PRESENT</code>.</li>\n<li>Everything you learnt about HashMap (buckets, collisions, resize, treeification) applies unchanged. See <em>Internal Working of HashMap</em>.</li>\n</ul>"
